#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""oma-runner: runs Oma's builders on the Mac Pro for the agent on the Fold. See docs/MAC-RUNNER-SPEC.md.

It listens on the Mac's Tailscale address only and every request must carry the key the phone holds. The Mac keeps
only the key's SHA-256 in ~/agents/claude-code/oma-runner.token, so an agent that reads that file cannot use it. It does one thing: run a coding agent (Claude Code, Codex or Cursor) in a
named project thread, under a folder Boss allowed, and stream what it says. It is not a shell: there is no endpoint that runs a command. It
approves nothing itself: every request for approval waits for the phone's answer, however long that takes.

  GET  /health                           {ok, working, waiting}
  GET  /threads                          every thread: id, name, summary, status, last_worked, tail, pending
  POST /threads   {name, project, kind, model, mode}   makes one under an allowed root; kind claude (default), codex or cursor;
                                         mode auto (default) or manual
  GET  /threads/ID                       one thread
  POST /threads/ID/messages   {text}     the owner's words; {taken: "now"} or {taken: "queued", position}
  GET  /threads/ID/stream[?after=N]      server-sent events from event N+1 on (or the Last-Event-ID header)
  GET  /threads/ID/events[?after=N]      the same events as plain JSON, for asking without holding a link
  POST /threads/ID/approvals/APPROVAL    {decision: "allow"|"deny", nonce}   answers that one request, once
  POST /threads/ID/stop                  stops the task and drops what was waiting its turn; what was said stays
  POST /files?name=NAME   (the bytes)    a file from the phone, kept in ~/Downloads/From Fold; {path, bytes}

Agent mail for a thread (tailshare's ~/.local/share/tailshare/mail/new/, to.session = the thread's id) is taken and
given to that thread labeled as another agent's, never as the owner's words.

  oma-runner serve | install | start | stop | status | rekey | uninstall
"""
import hashlib, hmac, json, os, pathlib, plistlib, re, secrets, select, shlex, shutil, signal, socket, subprocess, sys, tempfile, threading, time, tomllib, urllib.error, urllib.parse, urllib.request, uuid
